  <title>The programs it needs</title>
  <p>The bricks are programs that Virtualbricks runs: the VDE programs for the switches, the links and the interfaces of this computer, and QEMU for the virtual machines. On Debian and Ubuntu they come in these packages:</p>
  <table frame="all" rules="rows">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <p>Package</p>
        </td>
        <td>
          <p>Programs</p>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <p><sys>vde2</sys></p>
        </td>
        <td>
          <p><cmd>vde_switch</cmd>, <cmd>vde_plug</cmd>, <cmd>dpipe</cmd>, <cmd>vde_plug2tap</cmd>, <cmd>vde_pcapplug</cmd>, <cmd>vdeterm</cmd>, <cmd>unixterm</cmd></p>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <p><sys>vde2-cryptcab</sys></p>
        </td>
        <td>
          <p><cmd>vde_cryptcab</cmd>, for the tunnels</p>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <p><sys>vde-netemu</sys></p>
        </td>
        <td>
          <p><cmd>vde-netemu</cmd>, for the Netemu links</p>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <p><sys>qemu-system-x86</sys></p>
        </td>
        <td>
          <p><cmd>qemu-system-x86_64</cmd> and <cmd>qemu-system-i386</cmd></p>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <p><sys>qemu-system-arm</sys>, <sys>qemu-system-mips</sys>, <sys>qemu-system-ppc</sys>, <sys>qemu-system-riscv</sys>, <sys>qemu-system-s390x</sys>, <sys>qemu-system-sparc</sys>, <sys>qemu-system-misc</sys></p>
        </td>
        <td>
          <p>The other architectures, as <cmd>qemu-system-aarch64</cmd> in <sys>qemu-system-arm</sys>; on Ubuntu 24.04, <sys>qemu-system-misc</sys> has those of RISC-V</p>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <p><sys>qemu-utils</sys></p>
        </td>
        <td>
          <p><cmd>qemu-img</cmd>, which reads and makes the disk images</p>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The menu of the programs shows each once, by the name QEMU gives it: Debian's other names, as <cmd>qemu-system-arm64</cmd> for <cmd>qemu-system-aarch64</cmd>, only where a machine uses one. Some programs have no default machine type, as <cmd>qemu-system-aarch64</cmd> and <cmd>qemu-system-arm</cmd>: choose one in the section <gui>Machine</gui>, as <sys>virt</sys>, or the machine doesn't start.</p>
  <p>No distribution ships <cmd>vde_router</cmd>, the program of the router.</p>
  <section id="where">
    <title>Where Virtualbricks looks</title>
    <p>Each project has a folder for the QEMU programs and one for the VDE programs, in its settings: choose <guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Settings</gui></guiseq>, then the page of the project. A program that isn't in its folder is looked for in the folders of <sys>PATH</sys>.</p>
    <p>Under each folder, the <gui>Settings</gui> window says what it holds: the version of QEMU and its programs, and whether the programs of the bricks are there, or which one is missing and the package that has it. A folder that doesn't exist is an error, and <gui>OK</gui> stays greyed until it is fixed.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="missing">
    <title>When a program is missing</title>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p><gui>New Brick</gui> shows a warning sign on the kinds whose program is missing, and says which one.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>At start, Virtualbricks warns about the programs it can't find. <gui>Missing programs</gui>, in the settings of this computer, turns the warning off.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>A brick whose program is missing doesn't start, and the <gui>Logs</gui> window says why. Install the package, and start it again.</p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
